2153. The Number of Passengers in Each Bus II
2153. The Number of Passengers in Each Bus II
Table: Buses
Column Name	Type
bus_id	int
arrival_time	int

bus_id is the primary key of this table.

Each row contains information about a bus.

arrival_time represents the arrival time of the bus.

Table: Passengers
Column Name	Type
passenger_id	int
arrival_time	int

passenger_id is the primary key of this table.

Each row contains information about a passenger.

arrival_time represents the arrival time of the passenger.

Rules

Passengers board the earliest bus whose arrival_time is greater than or equal to the passenger’s arrival_time.

Each passenger can board only one bus.

If no bus arrives after a passenger arrives, the passenger does not board any bus.

Task

Write an SQL query to report the number of passengers that boarded each bus.

Return the result table ordered by bus_id in ascending order.

Example
Input

Buses

bus_id	arrival_time
1	2
2	4
3	7

Passengers

passenger_id	arrival_time
11	1
12	2
13	3
14	6
15	7
Output
bus_id	passengers_cnt
1	2
2	1
3	2

Solution 1
=========
WITH candidate AS (
  SELECT
    p.passenger_id,
    b.bus_id,
    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY p.passenger_id ORDER BY b.arrival_time) AS rn
  FROM Passengers p
  JOIN Buses b
    ON b.arrival_time >= p.arrival_time
)
, assigned AS (
  -- pick the earliest bus per passenger
  SELECT passenger_id, bus_id
  FROM candidate
  WHERE rn = 1
)
SELECT
  b.bus_id,
  COUNT(a.passenger_id) AS passengers_cnt
FROM Buses b
LEFT JOIN assigned a
  ON b.bus_id = a.bus_id
GROUP BY b.bus_id
ORDER BY b.bus_id;


Solution 2
=========
WITH bus_windows AS (
  SELECT
    bus_id,
    arrival_time,
    LAG(arrival_time) OVER (ORDER BY arrival_time) AS prev_time
  FROM Buses
)
SELECT
  b.bus_id,
  COUNT(p.passenger_id) AS passengers_cnt
FROM bus_windows b
LEFT JOIN Passengers p
  ON p.arrival_time <= b.arrival_time
 AND (b.prev_time IS NULL OR p.arrival_time > b.prev_time)
GROUP BY b.bus_id
ORDER BY b.bus_id;
